\section{Sharing Makes a Local Cost Ambiguous}

In an expression tree, finding the best tile at each goal is enough because the costs of the frontier subtrees are independent. In a directed acyclic graph, a single node can feed two or more consumers. If one tile absorbs the producer, it no longer supplies a result to the other consumers unless the instruction also exposes that result. The cost of materializing the producer is therefore a cost of the group of uses, not of any one edge. Unfolding the graph into a tree per result lets a tree matcher recover many legal covers of pure computation, but loses the information needed to charge a shared materialization once. It can also explode the size of the matching input when there are many paths to the same node.

In \emph{Near-Optimal Instruction Selection on DAGs}, Koes and Goldstein describe NOLTIS, a linear-time algorithm that builds on tree matching, and use an integer program as a comparison for optimality. They distinguish the tractable tree problem from the general DAG problem, which is NP-complete in their formulation. A practical selector can therefore use quick tree-based approximations for most blocks and reserve more expensive search for a small region rich in competing tiles. Treat compile-time budget and objective, such as code size, as separate choices.

\section{Worked Example: To Fold or to Share}

In a straight-line region, let a pure producer $p$ supply distinct consumers $f(p)$ and $g(p)$, both of whose outputs escape. Suppose the instruction for $p$ costs three, both ordinary consumer instructions cost one each, and each consumer has its own fused form of cost three that computes $p$ internally but does not return it. Looking only at $f$ gives a choice between four (produce then consume) and three (fuse); similarly for $g$. The sum of these independent choices is six. For the graph, computing $p$ once and using it twice costs five, while folding just one side costs seven because the other still needs the materialization.

For this example let $F,G$ be binary decisions to select the fused forms and $M$ be the decision to materialize $p$. The minimum of
\[
 3M+(1+2F)+(1+2G),\qquad
 M\geq 1-F,\quad M\geq 1-G
\]
is attained at $(F,G,M)=(0,0,1)$. A good regression changes the producer's cost from three to five, leaving the fused prices unchanged: the optimum now folds both. A second regression makes $p$ observable independently, which forces $M=1$ even if both folds are otherwise legal. Neither test alone establishes that duplicating $p$ is permitted; the example assumes $p$ is pure, with no trap or other visible effect.

\section{Tiles with Several Results}

Youn and colleagues' \emph{Fast Graph-Based Instruction Selection for Multi-Output Instructions} studies candidate identification and selection for operations that produce several results. Take an IR quotient and remainder of the same pair of inputs, and an instruction delivering both. Model one match with two output bindings, not two instructions that happen to share an opcode. The inputs must be in the same order and have the same signedness, and the operation must honor the IR's domain and exception behavior. If constrained output registers require copies, include the price of getting the two outputs to their actual uses. Two-result instructions are not automatically wins.

In a cover represented by decisions over candidate matches, each retained use needs a compatible definition that dominates it or is available on its incoming edge. Cover equations must account for which nodes a candidate implements internally and which values it exports. A superficially complete set of matches can still be unschedulable: merging members of an acyclic dependence graph can create a cycle in the contracted graph. For instance, absorbing $a$ and $c$ into one tile across a dependency path $a\to b\to c$ leaves one edge in each direction if $b$ still needs $a$ externally and the tile still needs $b$.

\section{Whole-Function Graphs}

Ebner and colleagues' \emph{Generalized Instruction Selection using SSA-Graphs} models complex patterns and memory dependencies in a graph grammar with a discrete optimizer. Whole-function selection does not make loop-carried inputs available before the loop: a $\phi$ operand is a use on one incoming edge and is subject to that edge's dominance rules. Also, separate arithmetic matches may be reorderable while an apparent fused load-and-update match is not, owing to aliasing or memory ordering. Keep dependency and placement checks explicit, and compare a heuristic's result on small graphs with an exact, legality-constrained reference. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/37-compiler-as-a-constraint-solver.md}{Compiler as a Constraint Solver} extends this engineering approach beyond selection.
